## Technology — Direct Conversion X-ray Imaging — https://athlos.fi/technology/direct-conversion.html

### What is direct conversion X-ray imaging?

Direct conversion X-ray imaging is a detector technology where X-ray photons are absorbed directly in a semiconductor material, such as CdTe or silicon, and converted into electrical charge. Unlike indirect conversion, it does not first convert X-rays into visible light in a scintillator. This removes the scintillator light-spread stage and helps preserve spatial accuracy, contrast and detail.

### Direct vs indirect conversion

- Signal path: direct = X-ray → electrical charge; indirect = X-ray → visible light (scintillator) → electrical charge.
- In indirect detectors, visible light can spread laterally in the scintillator before reaching the photodiode array, broadening the signal footprint. Columnar CsI mitigates but does not remove this stage.
- Direct conversion collects charge at local pixel electrodes near the absorption point — a shorter, more localized X-ray-to-signal pathway. Charge sharing between neighbouring pixels can still occur; direct conversion does not mean zero blur.
- Neither approach is universally better: indirect flat panels suit very large-area, cost-sensitive systems; direct conversion is strongest where sharpness, contrast resolution and dose efficiency drive the application.

### Athlos architectures

- CdTe-CMOS: CdTe bump-bonded to a full-custom CMOS ASIC — used in UFS and Industrial IP67 TDI (scanning, 10–360 kV).
- Si-CMOS: crystalline silicon integrated with CMOS readout — used in DC-Air® (compact intraoral imaging).

### Readout: charge integration, not photon counting

Direct conversion describes the conversion pathway; photon counting and charge integration describe readout modes. **Athlos sensors use direct conversion with charge-integrating (energy-integrating) readout. They are single-photon sensitive — the charge from a single X-ray photon is detectable above the noise floor — but they do NOT count individual photons or measure each photon's energy.** The team's 2005 photon-counting camera publication (NIM A 550) documents design heritage, not a current product feature.

### TDI scanning

TDI (Time Delay Integration) synchronizes line-detector readout with object motion; each of the 28 TDI stages in Athlos scanning sensors adds signal from the same object slice, improving signal-to-noise ratio at line rates up to 75,000 lps.

### Lineage (Simage → Ajat → Athlos)

1993 Simage Oy founded (Konstantinos Spartiotis, ex Johns Hopkins/CERN) — among the earliest direct conversion X-ray imaging companies. 1996–97 IEEE & CAR'97 papers: CdZnTe/Si pixel detectors flip-chip bonded to CMOS ASICs. 2001 Ajat Oy founded — global standard in CdTe-CMOS dental panoramic/cephalometric imaging. Peer-reviewed: NIM A 501 (2003) directly converting Si intraoral sensor (doi:10.1016/S0168-9002(03)00615-6); NIM A 527 (2004) CdTe real-time sensor; NIM A 550 (2005) photon-counting CdTe camera (doi:10.1016/j.nima.2005.04.081). 2017 Athlos Oy founded — DC-Air®, UFS, Industrial IP67 TDI. Historical specifications describe research devices, not current products.

